From 3cf337ff09c5aaf89516e319a2bfbdde91ccb628 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adrin Jalali <adrin.jalali@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 18:07:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] gh-152235: Track sets filled from an iterator ahead of the
 new elements

Since gh-152235, set(iterable), frozenset(iterable), set.union(),
set.intersection() and set.symmetric_difference() only GC-track the new
set once it is full, so that it can't be reached half-built.  When the
iterable creates the elements, as a generator does, the set then sits
behind them in the GC list: a full collection reaches the elements first,
parks them as tentatively unreachable and, once it reaches the set, moves
them back in hash order.  That scatters them in memory, and every later
full collection is up to ten times slower.

For such fills, track an empty set before the fill, fill a private
untracked set, and move the contents into the empty one with
set_swap_bodies() at the end.  The empty set can only be reached through
the GC; if it was kept, weakly referenced, hashed or changed meanwhile,
it is left as it is and the private set is returned instead.  This needs
no change to the GC.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
